{
  "why": [
    "One corpus, read from both sides of the wire, because two parsers have to agree about it and neither",
    "of them can check the other from where it runs.",
    "",
    "PyYAML is the authority: it is what `app.admin.apply.parse_document` runs, so `cluster` below is what",
    "the API server is actually sent. `backend/tests/test_yaml_scalar_divergence.py` asserts PyYAML still",
    "reads every row this way, which is what catches a PyYAML release that moves one.",
    "`frontend/tests/e2e/yaml-divergence.spec.js` asserts two things against the same rows: that js-yaml",
    "reads them as `browser`, and that the mirror schema in `frontend/src/components/yamlDivergence.js`",
    "reads them as `cluster`. A row where those two differ is a document this console would show one way",
    "and write another, and the editor warns about it.",
    "",
    "`browser` and `cluster` are the value written as JSON, with three spellings JSON has no literal for:",
    "\"@inf\", \"@-inf\" and \"@nan\". `diverges` is derived, not asserted -- it is there so a reader can see",
    "the shape of the set at a glance.",
    "",
    "One distinction is deliberately absent. Python has an int and a float where JavaScript has one number,",
    "so PyYAML reading `1.` as 1.0 and js-yaml reading it as 1 is a difference the mirror cannot represent",
    "and this warning does not claim to catch. It is also not the failure the warning is for: both are the",
    "number one, and no local check in the console reads them differently."
